The architecture is about <i>building for change</i>. Four properties it prioritizes:

1. <b>Independence of business logic</b> — the domain knows nothing about HTTP, databases, or specific frameworks.
2. <b>Testability</b> — domain logic can be tested with pure unit tests and no mocking infrastructure.
3. <b>Flexibility</b> — adapters can be swapped without touching the domain.
4. <b>Explicit boundaries</b> — interfaces between layers are visible, named, and reviewed.

### Directory structure

For a service crate at <code>rs/crates/services/<service-name>/</code>:

```javascript
src/
├── domain/           # core business logic
│   ├── mod.rs
│   ├── entities.rs   # domain entities + value objects
│   ├── ports.rs      # trait definitions (driving + driven)
│   ├── services.rs   # use cases / orchestration
│   └── errors.rs     # domain-specific error types
│
├── inbound/          # driving adapters (call into domain)
│   ├── mod.rs
│   ├── http/         # axum handlers
│   ├── grpc/         # tonic service impls
│   ├── cli/          # CLI commands
│   └── events/       # event consumers
│
├── outbound/         # driven adapters (called by domain via ports)
│   ├── mod.rs
│   ├── database/     # sqlx-based repositories
│   ├── http_client/  # reqwest-based external API clients
│   ├── messaging/    # message publishers/consumers
│   └── storage/      # object storage clients
│
├── config/           # configuration loading
│   ├── mod.rs
│   └── settings.rs
│
└── main.rs           # composition root
```

### Domain (<code>src/domain/</code>)

The core. Contains:

- <b>Entities</b> (<code>entities.rs</code>) — domain objects with behavior. Validation lives in their constructors.
- <b>Ports</b> (<code>ports.rs</code>) — trait definitions for everything the domain talks to (both directions: driving services and driven repositories).
- <b>Services</b> (<code>services.rs</code>) — use cases. Orchestrate entities through ports.
- <b>Errors</b> (<code>errors.rs</code>) — domain-specific typed error enum.

<b>Dependencies the domain may have:</b>

- Standard library and core Rust crates (<code>std</code>, basic utility crates).
- <code>paigasus-kernel</code> — the cross-language behavioral substrate is fair game. The kernel is itself pure logic with no adapters.
- <code>paigasus-proto</code> — usually OK if the domain naturally speaks the wire types. For complex services, prefer a translation layer between proto and domain types.

<b>Dependencies the domain must NOT have:</b>

- Anything in <code>src/inbound/</code> or <code>src/outbound/</code>.
- <code>sqlx</code>, <code>reqwest</code>, <code>axum</code>, <code>tonic</code>, or any adapter-specific crate.
- I/O, system time, randomness — inject those through ports if needed.

Example port definitions:

```rs
// src/domain/ports.rs
use crate::domain::entities::{User, UserId, Credentials, Session};
use crate::domain::errors::DomainError;

// Driven (outbound) port: the domain needs *something* to load and save users.
#[async_trait::async_trait]
pub trait UserRepository: Send + Sync {
    async fn find_by_id(&self, id: UserId) -> Result<User, DomainError>;
    async fn save(&self, user: User) -> Result<(), DomainError>;
}

// Driving (inbound) port: the use case inbound adapters call.
#[async_trait::async_trait]
pub trait AuthenticationService: Send + Sync {
    async fn authenticate(&self, credentials: Credentials) -> Result<Session, DomainError>;
}
```

Example entity with validation:

```rs
// src/domain/entities.rs
use thiserror::Error;
use paigasus_kernel::ids::Ulid;

#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum ValidationError {
    #[error("email address is invalid: {0}")]
    InvalidEmail(String),
    #[error("password must be at least 12 characters")]
    WeakPassword,
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct UserId(pub Ulid);

#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub struct EmailAddress(String);

impl EmailAddress {
    pub fn parse(s: impl Into<String>) -> Result<Self, ValidationError> {
        let s = s.into();
        if !s.contains('@') {
            return Err(ValidationError::InvalidEmail(s));
        }
        Ok(Self(s))
    }
    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &str { &self.0 }
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug)]
pub struct User {
    pub id: UserId,
    pub email: EmailAddress,
    pub password_hash: String,
}

impl User {
    pub fn new(email: String, password: String) -> Result<Self, ValidationError> {
        let email = EmailAddress::parse(email)?;
        let password_hash = Self::validate_and_hash_password(&password)?;
        Ok(Self { id: UserId(Ulid::generate()), email, password_hash })
    }

    fn validate_and_hash_password(password: &str) -> Result<String, ValidationError> {
        if password.len() < 12 {
            return Err(ValidationError::WeakPassword);
        }
        Ok(paigasus_kernel::crypto::hash_password(password))
    }
}
```

### Inbound (<code>src/inbound/</code>)

Driving adapters. Receive external input (HTTP requests, gRPC calls, CLI args, message events) and invoke the domain.

<b>Dependencies:</b> the domain (driving ports) and whatever framework crate the adapter uses (axum, tonic, clap, lapin, etc.).

<b>Pattern:</b> convert external types to domain types at the boundary, call the domain, convert the result back.

```rs
// src/inbound/http/auth.rs
use std::sync::Arc;
use axum::{Json, extract::State};
use serde::{Serialize, Deserialize};
use crate::domain::ports::AuthenticationService;
use crate::domain::entities::Credentials;

#[derive(Deserialize)]
pub struct LoginRequest {
    pub email: String,
    pub password: String,
}

impl LoginRequest {
    fn into_domain(self) -> Credentials {
        Credentials::new(self.email, self.password)
    }
}

#[derive(Serialize)]
pub struct SessionResponse {
    pub token: String,
    pub expires_at: String,
}

pub async fn login(
    State(auth): State<Arc<dyn AuthenticationService>>,
    Json(req): Json<LoginRequest>,
) -> Result<Json<SessionResponse>, ApiError> {
    let session = auth.authenticate(req.into_domain()).await?;
    Ok(Json(SessionResponse {
        token: session.token().to_string(),
        expires_at: session.expires_at().to_rfc3339(),
    }))
}
```

### Outbound (<code>src/outbound/</code>)

Driven adapters. Implement driven ports from the domain by talking to actual infrastructure.

<b>Dependencies:</b> the domain (the driven ports it implements) and the client crate it wraps (sqlx, reqwest, lapin, aws-sdk-s3, etc.).

<b>Pattern:</b> receive domain types, translate to infrastructure types, perform the operation, translate results back. Adapter-specific errors map to domain errors at the boundary.

```rs
// src/outbound/database/user_repository.rs
use async_trait::async_trait;
use sqlx::PgPool;
use crate::domain::entities::{User, UserId};
use crate::domain::errors::DomainError;
use crate::domain::ports::UserRepository;

pub struct PostgresUserRepository {
    pool: PgPool,
}

impl PostgresUserRepository {
    pub fn new(pool: PgPool) -> Self { Self { pool } }
}

#[async_trait]
impl UserRepository for PostgresUserRepository {
    async fn find_by_id(&self, id: UserId) -> Result<User, DomainError> {
        let row = sqlx::query_as::<_, UserRow>(
            "SELECT id, email, password_hash FROM users WHERE id = $1"
        )
        .bind(id.0.to_string())
        .fetch_one(&self.pool)
        .await
        .map_err(|e| match e {
            sqlx::Error::RowNotFound => DomainError::NotFound(format!("user {:?}", id)),
            _ => DomainError::Storage,  // hides sqlx-specific details
        })?;
        row.try_into_domain()
    }

    async fn save(&self, user: User) -> Result<(), DomainError> {
        let row = UserRow::from_domain(&user);
        sqlx::query(
            "INSERT INTO users (id, email, password_hash) VALUES ($1, $2, $3) \
             ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET email = EXCLUDED.email, \
             password_hash = EXCLUDED.password_hash"
        )
        .bind(row.id)
        .bind(row.email)
        .bind(row.password_hash)
        .execute(&self.pool)
        .await
        .map_err(|_| DomainError::Storage)?;
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

### Dependency rules

The fundamental invariant: <b>dependencies point inward, toward the domain</b>.

```javascript
┌──────────────────┐
│     Inbound      │ ──depends on─ Domain ports
│ (axum, tonic,    │
│  clap)           │
└──────────────────┘
         │
         ▼
┌──────────────────┐         paigasus-kernel
│      Domain      │ ─────── (allowed)
│ (entities,       │ ─────── paigasus-proto
│  ports, services)│         (allowed, with care)
└──────────────────┘
         ▲
         │
┌──────────────────┐
│     Outbound     │ ──implements─ Domain ports
│ (sqlx, reqwest,  │
│  s3)             │
└──────────────────┘
```

If you ever find yourself wanting domain code to import from <code>inbound/</code> or <code>outbound/</code>, that's the signal that a port is missing — invert the dependency by defining one.

### Error handling

Domain-specific typed errors prevent infrastructure details from leaking outward.

```rs
// src/domain/errors.rs
use thiserror::Error;
use crate::domain::entities::ValidationError;

#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum DomainError {
    #[error("not found: {0}")]
    NotFound(String),

    #[error("invalid credentials")]
    InvalidCredentials,

    #[error("validation failed: {0}")]
    Validation(#[from] ValidationError),

    #[error("storage error")]
    Storage,  // intentionally opaque — sqlx details don't escape
}
```

Adapters convert their native errors to <code>DomainError</code> at the boundary. The domain never sees <code>sqlx::Error</code>, <code>reqwest::Error</code>, or anything similar.

This dovetails with the <i>typed-errors-only across the FFI boundary</i> rule from [Polyglot Monorepo Scoping](https://www.notion.so/368830e8fbaa8101b0ffded7a3de3b53) § 3. Same principle, applied at a different boundary: any boundary that crosses contexts — adapter to domain, Rust to Python, OSS to private — uses typed errors, not opaque ones.

### Anti-patterns

<b>Adapter types in the domain.</b>

```rs
// BAD
pub trait UserRepository {
    async fn find_by_id(&self, id: UserId) -> Result<User, sqlx::Error>;
}
```

The domain just learned about sqlx. Now any swap of the database client breaks the domain. Use <code>DomainError</code>.

<b>Business logic in inbound handlers.</b>

```rs
// BAD: validation rule lives in the HTTP layer
pub async fn create_user(Json(req): Json<UserRequest>) -> Result<...> {
    if req.age < 18 { return Err(ApiError::TooYoung); }
    // ...
}
```

The rule "users must be 18+" is business logic. It belongs in <code>User::new()</code>. The HTTP handler doesn't get to enforce business invariants; the domain does.

<b>Adapters talking to adapters.</b>

```rs
// BAD: HTTP handler reaches directly into the database
pub async fn login(State(pool): State<PgPool>, Json(req): Json<LoginRequest>) {
    let user = sqlx::query("SELECT * FROM users").fetch_one(&pool).await?;
    // ...
}
```

The domain is bypassed. The composition root's job is to wire the HTTP handler to a domain service that talks to the repository through its port. The handler should not know that a database exists.

### Composition root (<code>main.rs</code>)

<code>main.rs</code> is the only place that knows how everything fits together. Its job, in order:

1. Initialize logging and observability.
2. Load configuration.
3. Construct outbound adapters (DB pools, HTTP clients).
4. Construct domain services, injecting outbound adapters by their port traits.
5. Construct inbound adapters, injecting the domain services.
6. Start serving.

```rs
// src/main.rs
use std::sync::Arc;
use crate::config::Settings;
use crate::domain::ports::AuthenticationService;
use crate::domain::services::AuthenticationServiceImpl;
use crate::outbound::database::PostgresUserRepository;
use crate::inbound::http::router;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    // 1. Logging / telemetry
    paigasus_logging::init()?;

    // 2. Config
    let settings = Settings::from_env()?;

    // 3. Outbound adapters
    let db_pool = sqlx::PgPool::connect(&settings.database_url).await?;
    let user_repo = Arc::new(PostgresUserRepository::new(db_pool));

    // 4. Domain services
    let auth: Arc<dyn AuthenticationService> = Arc::new(
        AuthenticationServiceImpl::new(user_repo.clone())
    );

    // 5. Inbound adapters
    let app = router(auth);
    let listener = tokio::net::TcpListener::bind(&settings.bind_address).await?;

    tracing::info!(address = %settings.bind_address, "gateway starting");
    axum::serve(listener, app).await?;
    Ok(())
}
```

This is dependency injection in plain Rust — no DI framework, no service locator. The graph is explicit and reviewable in one file.

### Testing strategy

The hex shape makes the testing tiers obvious:

- <b>Domain unit tests</b> — colocated in <code>src/domain/*</code> via <code>#[cfg(test)] mod tests</code>. Pure logic, no mocks needed. Fastest, most numerous, most maintainable.
- <b>Inbound adapter tests</b> — mock the driving ports the handler depends on. Verify HTTP/gRPC framing, deserialization, error translation. No real domain logic exercised here.
- <b>Outbound adapter tests</b> — integration tests using <code>testcontainers</code> (real Postgres, real Redis) or in-memory equivalents. Verify SQL, schema mappings, error translation.
- <b>End-to-end tests</b> — top-level <code>tests/</code> directory in the crate. Spin up the full service against test infrastructure; exercise representative user journeys.
- <b>Cross-binding tests</b> — for anything that lives in <code>paigasus-kernel</code>, the test suite runs against the Rust impl AND each FFI binding to catch behavioral drift. See [Polyglot Monorepo Scoping](https://www.notion.so/368830e8fbaa8101b0ffded7a3de3b53) § 3.

### Incremental adoption

For greenfield Paigasus services, just start with hex from day one — the structure costs little and pays off immediately. For any service that grew organically without the structure (or for imported legacy code), don't rewrite. Instead:

1. Extract the domain logic into <code>src/domain/</code> without changing behavior. Pull out value objects, named types, validation rules.
2. Define ports as traits that capture what the existing code depends on.
3. Move adapter code into <code>src/inbound/</code> and <code>src/outbound/</code>, implementing the ports.
4. Refactor <code>main.rs</code> into a proper composition root.

One module at a time. The codebase stays shippable at every step.

### Key takeaways

1. The domain is the hub. Adapters orbit it. Dependencies always flow inward.
2. Traits define contracts (ports). Concrete types in adapters implement them.
3. Convert at every boundary — external types to domain types on the way in, domain types to external types on the way out.
4. Validation happens in domain entities. Their constructors are the gatekeepers.
5. Errors are typed; adapter-specific errors never escape adapters.
6. The composition root is the only place the full dependency graph is visible.
7. Hex isn't free — apply it where its properties matter, skip it where they don't.

